i2c: sifive: one address phase per transfer, not per message

<zephyr/drivers/i2c.h> says adjacent message fragments without I2C_MSG_RESTART belong to
one transaction. i2c_sifive.c sends START + address for EVERY message, so i2c_burst_write()
-- one message for the register byte, one for the payload -- puts a repeated START in the
middle. drivers/display/ssd1306.c uses i2c_burst_write_dt() for every command and every
framebuffer write, and an SSD1306 reads the first byte after a (repeated) START as its
control byte. The result is a garbled screen, not an error: every byte is ACKed.

MEASURED, on the generated TLI2C in Verilator with this driver compiled unmodified
(samples/oled_status/sim/tli2c, scripts/55_oled_rtl_sim.sh --tli2c; the RTL is a copy of
PynqZ2RocketBigLittlePextTacitMicRgbRoccMoonCamConfig's TLI2C.sv, which is byte-identical
to PynqZ2RocketTacitCamConfig's but for the monitor's instance name):

  stock driver    a 128x64 screen puts 10 repeated STARTs on the wire, 11 control bytes
                  are misparsed, 9 of 1,024 framebuffer bytes reach the GDDRAM, and the
                  rendered image does not match the host golden. Every transfer returns 0.
  with this patch one START per transfer, 0 repeated STARTs, 1,024 data bytes, and the
                  image matches the host golden at both 100 kHz and 400 kHz.

WHY THE ARTY-200T STACK NEVER HIT THIS. Nothing in the software that drove I2C there calls
a burst API: the riskybird samples (ads7128, vl53l5cx, sensor_bringup, tof, i2c_expander),
the in-tree BMI08x and VL53L1X drivers and the HM01B0 camera driver all use single-buffer
i2c_write() or i2c_write_read(), which this driver already handles correctly. The SSD1306
driver is the first user of i2c_burst_write() on this controller.

The rule implemented here is the one drivers/i2c/i2c_bitbang.c and drivers/i2c/i2c_dw.c
follow: address phase on the first message, after a STOP, on I2C_MSG_RESTART, or on a
change of direction. The read path's last-byte NACK moves with it, so a read continued by
a following fragment is still acknowledged.

Nothing else changes: single-message writes and write-then-read (the camera's two shapes)
produce exactly the same bytes as before.
